.TH NVME_TP_MONITOR 1 "September 2026" "ebpf-syscall" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nvme_tp_monitor \- capture NVMe driver commands and completion latency
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nvme_tp_monitor
.RB [ \-\-dur " SECONDS" ]
.RB [ \-\-jsonl " PATH" ]
.RB [ \-\-disk " NVME_NAMESPACE" ]
.RB [ \-\-lba-size " BYTES" ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nvme_tp_monitor
attaches to Linux NVMe command and completion tracepoints.  It records the
ordered operation, namespace, LBA, byte length, monotonic issue time, hardware
queue, command ID, completion status, and completion latency.  A final drop
record lets consumers detect capture loss.
.PP
This is the device witness used by
.BR "kvio record" .
It sees commands from every submission API that reaches the selected NVMe
driver, including IO not issued by kvio.
.SH OBSERVATION POINT
This monitor attaches to
.B nvme_setup_cmd
and
.B nvme_complete_rq
inside the Linux NVMe driver.  These hooks are below syscall and io_uring
batching.  If an application fills the shared ring with 64 SQEs and submits
them with one
.BR io_uring_enter (2)
call, a generic syscall monitor sees one call while the driver hooks run for 64
requests and 64 completions.  A loss-free capture records all 64.  This tree's
.BR syscall_monitor (1)
does not hook that enter call at all.  SQPOLL may consume SQEs without any new
system call while its kernel poll thread remains awake.
.PP
The captured command is the command Linux built for the namespace.  This is
not a PCIe-wire capture and does not expose controller firmware, FTL or NAND
operations.  It also cannot see SPDK/VFIO or another userspace-owned controller
path that bypasses the Linux NVMe driver.
.PP
At setup, the BPF program emits command metadata and stores the issue timestamp
in a hash keyed by
.BR "struct request *" .
The completion hook looks up and deletes that entry, computes latency in the
kernel, and emits the completion record through the ring buffer.  Require the
final loss record to be zero before calling the stream complete.
.PP
The tracepoints do not carry an io_uring
.B user_data
cookie.  Use kvio's offset-and-time join to attribute a command to an object,
or use
.BR nvme_uring_cmd_monitor (1)
when passthrough-cookie correlation is required.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-dur " seconds"
Stop after the given duration.  Otherwise stop with SIGINT or SIGTERM.
.TP
.BI \-\-jsonl " path"
Write newline-delimited JSON to the path instead of standard output.
.TP
.BI \-\-disk " namespace"
Capture only the named block namespace, for example
.BR nvme1n1 .
The monitor discovers its logical block size from sysfs.  Filtering is
required for an unambiguous portable replay capture.  Pass the whole namespace
name, not a partition such as
.BR nvme1n1p1 .
.TP
.BI \-\-lba-size " bytes"
Supply a positive power-of-two logical block size.  This is required when
.B \-\-disk
is omitted because several observed namespaces can have different geometry.
.SH OUTPUT AND PRIVACY
Command records contain IO metadata, not payload bytes, file names, KV object
keys, graph nodes, prompts, or tensors.  Completion records contain status,
latency, queue, command ID, and time.
.PP
Payload-free does not mean anonymous.  Exact LBAs reveal locality and device
range, timing reveals request cadence, and disk identity or an unusual shape
can identify a system or workload.  Minimize and review captures before
sharing them.
.SH FIDELITY
Require a final
.B dropped: 0
record for an exact-stream claim.  Use
.B kvio iolog
to export the requested stream, then capture the fio replay with this monitor
and use
.B kvio compare
to check ordered operations, byte offsets and lengths, issue timing, and
completion-latency distributions.
.PP
Completion records do not carry disk identity.  Consumers pair them using
hardware queue, command ID, and time ordering or use their latency
distribution.  kvio refuses one-to-one latency comparison when command-ID
reuse overlaps, a completion is missing or orphaned, or its recorded latency
disagrees with the command and completion timestamps.
.SH TOPOLOGY LIMITS
The tracepoint observes requests at an NVMe leaf namespace.  IO submitted
through a partition, filesystem, device-mapper, LVM, or software RAID has
already been mapped, split, merged, or reordered.  The capture describes the
resulting namespace-relative device commands; it cannot reconstruct the
upper-layer address or redundancy semantics.
.PP
The schema records the kernel disk name and NVMe namespace ID, but not native
NVMe multipath controller selection, ANA state, failover, or transport
identity.  It therefore cannot certify path behavior.  Capture each namespace
separately.  A single LBA size cannot decode an unfiltered capture spanning
namespaces with different formats, and kvio rejects that capture as replay
input.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kvio (1),
.BR syscall_monitor (1),
.BR nvme_uring_cmd_monitor (1),
.BR iouring_monitor (1)
